course: everyday
lesson: 297
title: Phone calls, visiting, thank-you notes, interrupting and quiet places
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Good manners are mostly about noticing other people: what they own, what they feel and what they are trying to do. A friend's house, a cinema, a museum or a place of worship each has its own way of showing that respect.
explain: When you visit a friend, you are a guest. Stay in the rooms you are invited into, leave cupboards closed, and help tidy up any mess you made before you go, so the house is left as you found it.
explain: A present is someone's time, thought and money. Saying thank you soon tells the giver it arrived safely and that you liked it. A short message is fine for small things, but a handwritten card for a big gift shows real effort and is often kept.
explain: Example: a boy in Denver wants to tell his mum something while she talks to a neighbour. He waits for a pause, or rests his hand gently on her arm so she knows he is waiting. If it really cannot wait, he says "Excuse me" first.
explain: In quiet places, your noise or touch can spoil things for others: rustling wrappers in a film, running in a mosque, touching an old painting, or chatting at a funeral.
fact: If a number you do not know calls or messages you, let a parent answer or ignore it, because unknown callers can be scammers.
fact: At a friend's house, stay in the rooms you are invited into; other rooms and cupboards are private.
fact: If you made a mess while playing at someone's house, help tidy up before you go home.
fact: When a present arrives by post, phone, message or write soon so the giver knows it arrived and that you liked it.
fact: For birthday or holiday presents, write a short thank-you note or card to each giver within a week or two.
fact: An email or message is fine to thank someone for small things, but a handwritten card means more for big gifts.
fact: To get a parent's attention while they talk, wait for a pause or put your hand gently on their arm and wait.
fact: If you really have to interrupt, say "Excuse me" or "Sorry to interrupt" first.
fact: At the cinema, eat quietly and do not rustle wrappers, because noise spoils the film for others.
fact: In a church, mosque, synagogue, temple or gurdwara (a Sikh place of worship), speak quietly, do not run, and follow its rules, such as covering your head or taking off your shoes.
fact: During a service, sit quietly, stand or sit when others do if you are comfortable, and take no photos unless allowed.
fact: In a museum or gallery, walk calmly and do not touch exhibits unless a sign says you may, because many are old and fragile.
fact: At a funeral or memorial, dress neatly, stay quiet and follow the family's lead, because people are sad.
exercise: Your tablet shows a message from a number you do not know. What should you do? | Let a parent see it, or ignore it | Reply to ask who they are | Call the number back to check | Send them your name so they know you
exercise: Why should children not answer calls from unknown numbers? | The caller could be a scammer | Calls from new numbers cost a lot | Phones break if you answer strangers | It is rude to answer quickly
exercise: At a friend's house you are curious about the other rooms. What is polite? | Stay in the rooms you are invited into | Look around the house while your friend is busy | Peek in but do not touch anything | Open cupboards to find more toys
exercise: You and your friend built a huge den with every cushion in the lounge. It is time to go home. What should you do? | Help tidy it up before you leave | Leave it, since it is their house | Tell your friend to tidy it later | Leave quickly before anyone notices
exercise: Your aunt in Canada posts you a book for your birthday. What should you do? | Phone, message or write soon to say thank you | Wait until you see her again next year, then thank her in person | Thank her only if you liked the book | Nothing, since the post shows it arrived
exercise: Why does a giver like to hear from you after posting a present? | They want to know it arrived and that you liked it | They want to check you paid the postage | They want the present sent back to them if you don't like it | They need to know your new address
exercise: You got six birthday presents. When should you send thank-you notes? | Within a week or two, one to each giver | Only to the one who gave the biggest present | At next year's birthday | Only if a giver asks for one
exercise: Which way of saying thank you means most for a big gift from your grandparents? | A handwritten card | A quick emoji in a message | A thumbs-up on a video call | Telling your friend to tell them
exercise: Your mum is talking to your teacher and you want to tell her something. What should you do? | Wait for a pause, or put your hand gently on her arm and wait | Pull her sleeve again and again until she stops and turns round to you | Talk loudly so she hears you at once | Stand in front of her and wave
exercise: You really have to interrupt two adults because your brother has fallen over. How should you start? | Say "Excuse me" first | Just start talking straight away | Tap them hard on the shoulder | Shout their names to stop them
exercise: You have a crunchy snack in a crisp packet at the cinema. What is polite? | Eat quietly and do not rustle the packet | Eat it all fast at the loudest part | Open it very slowly during the quietest bits so nobody hears | Pass it along the row to share
exercise: You visit a gurdwara with a friend's family. What should you do? | Speak quietly, follow the rules like covering your head and taking off shoes | Run around to see everything | Keep your shoes and cap on to keep clean and warm, since the floors are cold and dirty | Talk normally as everyone is busy
exercise: You are at a friend's church during a service. Which is respectful? | Sit quietly and take no photos unless it is allowed | Take lots of photos to show your family when you get home later | Play a game on your phone quietly | Walk around to look at the windows
exercise: In a museum you see an old vase with no sign about touching. What should you do? | Look but do not touch it | Touch it gently, since there is no sign | Pick it up to see the bottom | Tap it to hear if it is real
exercise: Why are museum exhibits usually not to be touched? | Many are old and fragile | They are covered in paint | They are all made of glass | Touching makes the alarm ring every time
exercise: You go to a funeral for a neighbour. What should you do? | Dress neatly, stay quiet and follow the family's lead | Wear your brightest party clothes | Chat and tell jokes to cheer everyone up a bit | Leave early if you feel bored
test: A girl in Leeds gets a text: "You won a prize! Reply with your address." What should she do? | Show a parent and not reply | Reply, since prizes are good | Send only her first name | Forward it to her friends
test: Which is a good guest at a friend's house? | One who stays in the invited rooms and helps tidy up | One who explores every room in the whole house | One who leaves the mess for the parents | One who opens the fridge without asking
test: A boy in Texas got money from his uncle and sent nothing back for three months. What would have been better? | A thank-you note within a week or two | Spending it straight away and saying nothing at all | Asking for more next time | Waiting until his uncle asked
test: Which is true about thanking people? | A message is fine for small things, but a card means more for big gifts | Messages are always rude, so only ever send a card | Only gifts you like need thanks | Handwritten cards are only for adults
test: Your dad is on an important phone call. You want a snack. What do you do? | Wait for him to finish or for a pause | Talk over him to ask | Take the phone off him to ask for it yourself | Bang on the table so he looks up
test: Why should you say "Excuse me" before you interrupt? | It makes the interruption polite | It means you can then talk as long as you like | It is only needed with teachers | It stops the other person being cross forever
test: In a temple, the people take off their shoes at the door. What should a visitor do? | Take off their shoes too | Keep them on, since they are a visitor | Ask why and keep them on | Carry them inside in their hands
test: Why is it important to be quiet at a funeral? | People are sad and need respect | Funerals are always held in libraries | Noise is against the law there | The service is recorded for television
test: At a gallery a sign says "Please touch" next to a sculpture. What is right? | You may touch that one gently | You must still never touch it | You may touch every work in the room | You may climb on it
test: Which is the most polite at the cinema? | Unwrapping sweets before the film starts | Unwrapping sweets in the quietest scene | Talking quietly about the plot as it happens on screen | Kicking the seat in front for fun
test: unknown number keeps calling me | Let a parent answer it or ignore it | Answer and give your name | Call it back to find out who keeps ringing | Text them your address to check
test: nan sent me a parcel, it came today | Let her know soon it arrived and that you liked it | Wait till you see her next year, she won't mind | No need, she knows the post works | Only say thanks if you like it
test: mum's on the phone n i need her NOW (not an emergency) | Wait for a pause or put a hand on her arm | Shout until she hangs up | Pull the phone away from her ear so she listens | Keep tapping her shoulder hard
test: at the museum, can i touch the old vase | No, don't touch unless a sign says you may | Yes, gently with one finger | Yes, if no guard is looking that way | Only if it's behind glass
explain: A boy in New York picked up a call from an unknown number and told the caller his address and that his parents were out; his mum was very worried and they told the police.
explain: A girl in Wales opened her friend's mum's cupboard while visiting and broke a precious vase; she had not been invited in there, and her friend was not allowed to have visitors for a while.
fact: Strangers sometimes call or message children to find out where they live or when they are alone.
fact: Unknown numbers are best answered by a parent, or not at all.
fact: If a caller asks whether your parents are home, never say you are alone; say they can't come to the phone, hang up and tell them.
test: unknown number calling, asks if my parents are home | Say they can't come to the phone and hang up, then tell them | Say no, you're alone | Give your address | Ask who they are and have a chat
test: at mates house, can i check his big sisters room | No, stay in the rooms you're invited into | Yes, if she's out | Yes, if you're careful | Yes, if your mate says it's fine
test: library dude shushed us lol | Be quiet; others are reading and studying | Be louder, it's a free country and you can talk | Leave and come back louder | Laugh at him
test: did u hear?? a girl answered a random number and told them she was home alone, "and then a car parked outside all night". lesson? | Let a parent answer unknown numbers, or ignore them | Answer but don't say your name | Unknown callers are always friendly | Only answer if it's the daytime and you're bored
test: my mate says a kid touched a painting in the museum and "his parents had to pay a million pounds". what's prob exaggerated | The million; but don't touch exhibits, they're old and fragile | That museums have old paintings | That exhibits can be fragile | That signs say don't touch
test: kids are saying the girl who never sends thank-you cards is "spoilt and ungrateful". pass it on? | No, it's unkind gossip; just remember to thank people yourself | Yes, she is spoilt | Yes, and stop giving her presents until she learns some manners | Yes, and tell her parents
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the treatment of telephone calls from unknown numbers to a child? | They shall be answered by a parent, or not at all | They shall be answered promptly and in full by the child | They shall be answered with one's address | They shall be returned later the same day
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a thank-you message is sent when a present arrives by post. | So the giver knows it arrived and that it was liked | So the postal service may record its delivery | So that a further present may be requested | So the parcel may be returned if unwanted
exam: It is given that a child wishes to attract a parent's attention whilst the parent is speaking. Select the response consistent with courtesy. | Wait for a pause or rest a hand gently upon the arm and wait | Speak louder and louder than the parent until one is heard | Pull repeatedly upon the parent's sleeve | Stand directly between the speakers
exam: State the conduct expected within a place of worship such as a church, mosque, synagogue, temple or gurdwara. | Speaking quietly, not running and following its rules | Speaking freely and taking photographs at will, as in any public place | Running in order to find a seat quickly | Ignoring its rules if one does not share the faith
